+# App-Wide Form-Binding Hardening Is Deferred to a Dedicated Slice
+
+**Status:** Accepted
+
+## Context
+
+The security review of `REQ-VIS-003` found mass assignment on `VisitController#processVisitCorrectionForm`. The handler binds request parameters onto the `Owner` it needs only as a save target. A crafted POST to the visit-edit route writes owner and pet fields the form never offered.
+
+The same shape was already present on three sibling handlers before the slice: `VisitController#processNewVisitForm`, `PetController#processCreationForm`, and `PetController#processUpdateForm`. `OwnerController#processUpdateOwnerForm` binds `Owner` too, but there the owner is the form's own subject.
+
+The review rated the finding LOW and approved the slice. Three facts bound the exposure. No privilege boundary is crossed, because the application has no authentication and the same unauthenticated writes are already reachable on the owner-edit and pet-edit routes. Every binder in the three controllers disallows `id` and `*.id`, so the bound graph cannot be repointed at another owner's rows. `Owner`'s bean-validation constraints are enforced before persistence.
+
+## Options Considered
+
+1. **Fix the new handler only** — add `@ModelAttribute(binding = false)` to `processVisitCorrectionForm` inside `REQ-VIS-003`.
+2. **Close the class app-wide inside this slice** — change all affected handlers while implementing the visit correction.
+3. **Ship as reviewed and record the class as an open risk** — leave the code unchanged and defer the hardening to its own slice.
+
+## Decision
+
+We take option 3. Option 1 would diverge one handler from four siblings while leaving the class open, which makes the codebase harder to reason about without reducing exposure. Option 2 folds an app-wide security change into a feature slice, mixing two independent behaviors in one review and one commit.
+
+The product owner's standing instruction for this request is the narrowest reading consistent with the request, with open questions recorded rather than waited on. The hardening is therefore out of this request's scope and is recorded as an open risk for a human to schedule.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- Five handlers keep the incidental-binding shape, and the exposure is unchanged by `REQ-VIS-003`.
+- The risk is discoverable in [`system-design.md` § Deferred Risks](../system-design.md#deferred-risks) with its route set and proposed remedy, so the next engineer meets it before touching these controllers.
+- The deferral holds only while the application has no authentication. Adding authentication turns this class into a privilege-boundary crossing and raises its severity.
+- The hardening slice, when scheduled, changes all five handlers; `OwnerController#processUpdateOwnerForm` needs a treatment of its own, given in [`system-design.md` § Deferred Risks](../system-design.md#deferred-risks).
+
+## Implementation
+
+**Requirements:** REQ-VIS-003
+
+## References
+
+- [`system-design.md` § Deferred Risks](../system-design.md#deferred-risks)
+- [`system-design.md` § Security Context](../system-design.md#security-context) — the absent-authentication posture this deferral rests on
+- [`security-principles.md`](../security-principles.md) — the mass-assignment row of the Realization table
